# Model

This folder contains the EMBER2024 malware classification and clustering pipeline.

## Setup

```bash
git clone https://github.com/FutureComputing4AI/EMBER2024.git
cd EMBER2024 && pip install .
cd ..
pip install lightgbm scikit-learn hdbscan umap-learn tqdm huggingface_hub
```

## Run

**Test run (PDF subset, ~200 MB):**

```bash
python model/ember2024_pipeline.py --data-dir ./ember_data --file-type PDF
```

**Limit training data to 1 GB:**

```bash
python model/ember2024_pipeline.py --data-dir ./ember_data --file-type PDF --max-gb 1
```

**Skip download if data is already on disk:**

```bash
python model/ember2024_pipeline.py --data-dir ./ember_data --skip-download --max-gb 1
```

**Full dataset:**

```bash
python model/ember2024_pipeline.py --data-dir ./ember_data --file-type all --max-gb 1
```

**Evaluate trained models:**

```bash
python model/evaluate_models.py
```

## Output Files

| File                           | Description                              |
| ------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------- |
| `lgbm_classifier.model`        | LightGBM malicious/benign classifier     |
| `random_forest_classifier.pkl` | Random Forest classifier                 |
| `kmeans_clusterer.pkl`         | K-Means clustering model                 |
| `hdbscan_clusterer.pkl`        | HDBSCAN clustering model                 |
| `cluster_scaler.pkl`           | Scaler used for clustering preprocessing |
| `cluster_pca.pkl`              | PCA used for clustering preprocessing    |
| `training_report.txt`          | Metrics summary from last training run   |
| `evaluation.png`               | Visual evaluation charts                 |

## Model Performance

Models were trained on 1 GB of PDF file data from the EMBER2024 dataset and evaluated on 24,000 test files (12,000 benign, 12,000 malicious).

### LightGBM (best model)

| Metric              | Score  | Meaning                                                         |
| ------------------- | ------ | --------------------------------------------------------------- |
| AUC                 | 0.9913 | How well it ranks malicious above benign (max = 1.0)            |
| Accuracy            | 0.955  | Correctly classified 95.5% of all files                         |
| Precision           | 0.993  | When it flags a file as malicious, it's right 99.3% of the time |
| Recall              | 0.924  | Catches 92.4% of all malicious files                            |
| F1                  | 0.957  | Combined precision + recall score                               |
| False Positive Rate | 0.007  | Only 0.7% of benign files are wrongly flagged                   |
| False Negative Rate | 0.075  | 7.5% of malicious files slip through                            |

### Random Forest

| Metric              | Score  | Meaning                                            |
| ------------------- | ------ | -------------------------------------------------- |
| AUC                 | 0.9891 | Slightly behind LightGBM                           |
| Accuracy            | 0.950  | Correctly classified 95.0% of all files            |
| Precision           | 0.994  | When it flags a file, it's right 99.4% of the time |
| Recall              | 0.904  | Catches 90.4% of all malicious files               |
| F1                  | 0.947  | Combined precision + recall score                  |
| False Positive Rate | 0.005  | Fewer false alarms than LightGBM                   |
| False Negative Rate | 0.096  | Misses more malicious files than LightGBM          |

### Confusion Matrix Breakdown (LightGBM, 24,000 test files)

|                        | Predicted Benign | Predicted Malicious |
| ---------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------- |
| **Actually Benign**    | 11,918 ✅        | 82 ❌ (false alarm) |
| **Actually Malicious** | 906 ❌ (missed)  | 11,094 ✅           |

**LightGBM is the recommended model** — it catches more malware (higher recall) at the cost of slightly more false alarms, which is the right tradeoff for a security tool. Performance is expected to improve further when trained on the full dataset.

## Available File Types

| Type  | Size    | Notes                      |
| ----- | ------- | -------------------------- |
| PDF   | ~200 MB | Smallest, good for testing |
| ELF   | ~100 MB | Even smaller               |
| APK   | ~1.2 GB | Android apps               |
| Win32 | ~28 GB  | Windows 32-bit executables |
| Win64 | ~15 GB  | Windows 64-bit executables |
| all   | ~50+ GB | Full dataset               |
